<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements for the Mezan main bundle (Developer ID
  distribution, NOT the Mac App Store).

  `apps/desktop/electron-builder.yml` names this file explicitly and sets
  `mac.hardenedRuntime: true`. Hardened runtime is what Apple requires before
  it will notarize anything, and notarization is what lets Gatekeeper say the
  installer is provably ours — the whole point of the signing work. Under
  hardened runtime the process loses several capabilities by default and gets
  them back only by naming them here, so this file is a privilege grant and is
  read as one.

  WHAT IS GRANTED, AND WHY EXACTLY ONE THING IS:

  com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit — V8 compiles JavaScript to machine code at
  runtime and needs pages it may both write and execute. Without this the
  renderer dies on launch. There is no version of this product that does not
  need it.

  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT GRANTED, because @electron/osx-sign's own default
  entitlements hand out several of these and a finance application should not
  inherit a permissive default it never asked for:

  - com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — would let the process
    load code signed by a DIFFERENT team. This build ships one native module
    (better-sqlite3), electron-builder signs it with OUR identity along with
    everything else in the bundle, so library validation passes on its own.
    Disabling it would mean an attacker who can drop a .dylib beside the app
    gets it loaded into the process that holds the shop's books.
  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory — a strictly wider
    grant than allow-jit, and Electron has not needed it since v20.
  - com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables — would let anyone
    who can set DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES inject into a launch of this app.
  - device.camera / device.audio-input / device.usb / device.bluetooth /
    personal-information.location — osx-sign's default plist grants all five.
    This is an accounting and inventory product. It uses none of them, and a
    grant nobody uses is a grant nobody notices being abused.

  NOTE ON THE APP SANDBOX: com.apple.security.app-sandbox and the
  com.apple.security.network.* pair are absent on purpose. They are Mac App
  Store requirements; Developer ID + hardened runtime does not use the sandbox,
  and a sandboxed build could not bind the LAN multi-till socket
  (apps/desktop/src/main/lan-host.ts) or write backups outside its container
  (main/env.ts `defaultBackupsDir`). If this product is ever submitted to the
  Mac App Store that is a separate `mas` target with its own plist, not an edit
  to this one.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
</plist>
